  <link rel="alternate"
        type="application/json+oembed"
        href="http://umap.openstreetmap.de/map/oembed/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fumap.openstreetmap.de%2Fde%2Fmap%2Fopenbuildingmap_81684&format=json"
        title="OpenBuildingMap oEmbed URL" />
  <meta property="og:url" content="https://umap.openstreetmap.de/de/map/openbuildingmap_81684" />
  <meta property="og:title" content="OpenBuildingMap" />
  <meta property="og:description" content="Buildings play a critical role in understanding human settlement patterns and are essential for processes in crisis manage-ment, urban planning, energy efficiency, and multi-hazard risk assessment. To address the need for comprehensive andaccessible global building data, we introduce a dataset containing 2.7 billion building footprints classified using the buildingtaxonomy of the Global Earthquake Model. This dataset is created by conflatingthe AI-derived Google Open Buildings and the Microsoft Global ML Building Footprints datasets, and the crowdsourcedOpenStreetMap, hence creating the most detailed and extensive building dataset to date. These AI-derived building footprints help balancing out the complete-ness bias in OpenStreetMap in which mapping is most complete in countries with high human developing index, althoughmajor gaps remain in certain countries and rural areas. In addition, occupancy types and building height estimation wasvalidated through distribution comparison using Kullback-Leibler divergence across specific cities, and through cadaster datafrom Slovenia and Greece. The quality and completeness of the resulting dataset is similar to the cadaster datasets, revealingthat, while some misclassifications occur due to definitional differences or data limitations, the dataset overall provides reliableand valuable building information at global scale. Among other applications, one could reuse the data to identify vulnerabilitiesof buildings for natural hazards, to model population distributions based, or to calculate carbon footprints of buildings.

The data paper is accessible via [[https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-025-06132-z]].
The data publication is accessible via [[https://doi.org/10.5880/GFZ.LKUT.2025.002]].
" />
  <meta property="og:site_name" content="uMap" />

